{"threeImage": {"scenes": [{"sceneId": 0, "sceneType": "360", "showBackButton": true, "iconType": "arrow", "scenename": "The church of the Solitudine", "scenesrc": {"path": "../content/images/scena-01.jpg", "mime": "image/jpeg", "copyright": {"license": "U"}, "width": 4096, "height": 2048}, "scenedescription": "", "cameraStartPosition": "-1.7592918860102849,0.0", "interactions": [{"interactionpos": "0.09424777960769504,-0.06283185307179592", "action": {"library": "H5P.GoToScene 0.1", "params": {"nextSceneId": 1}, "subContentId": "56ae257f-7100-4d4b-89d5-7e8a088c35d1", "metadata": {"contentType": "Go To Scene", "license": "U", "title": "To the avenue"}}, "label": {"labelPosition": "inherit", "showLabel": "inherit"}, "labelText": "To the avenue"}, {"interactionpos": "-1.7592918860102849,0.31415926535897926", "action": {"library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1", "params": {"text": "<p>The church of the Madonna della Solitudine stands in Nuoro, at the foot of Mount Ortobene. The first country sanctuary dates back to the seventeenth century; the present building, simple and bare, in grey granite, was rebuilt in the twentieth century to a design by the Nuoro artist Giovanni Ciusa Romagna. Grazia Deledda, winner of the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1926, set the last novel published in her lifetime, <em>La chiesa della solitudine</em> (1936), around this church. Since 1959 it has held her remains, brought here from Rome, in a black sarcophagus.</p><h3>The bronze door</h3><p>The entrance to the church is closed by a bronze door made by the Sassari-born sculptor Eugenio Tavolara (1901–1963), who also created part of the church&#x27;s liturgical furnishings. In the centre is the Madonna enthroned with the Child, surrounded by human figures, animals and symbols.</p>"}, "subContentId": "fc651c2c-91ca-48f5-bcf5-17db28d90763", "metadata": {"contentType": "Text", "license": "U", "title": "The church of the Solitudine"}}, "label": {"labelPosition": "inherit", "showLabel": "inherit"}, "labelText": "The church of the Solitudine"}, {"interactionpos": "-1.5393804002589988,0.09424777960769387", "action": {"library": "H5P.Image 1.1", "params": {"decorative": false, "contentName": "Image", "expandImage": "Expand image", "minimizeImage": "Minimize image", "file": {"path": "../content/images/porta-bronzo.jpg", "mime": "image/jpeg", "copyright": {"license": "U"}, "width": 387, "height": 516}, "alt": "The bronze door of the church of the Solitudine"}, "subContentId": "2ffcfb11-8397-4a34-b8a2-1368da25904d", "metadata": {"contentType": "Image", "license": "U", "title": "The bronze door"}}, "label": {"labelPosition": "inherit", "showLabel": "inherit"}, "labelText": "The bronze door"}]}, {"sceneId": 1, "sceneType": "360", "showBackButton": true, "iconType": "arrow", "scenename": "The avenue", "scenesrc": {"path": "../content/images/scena-02.jpg", "mime": "image/jpeg", "copyright": {"license": "U"}, "width": 4096, "height": 2048}, "scenedescription": "", "cameraStartPosition": "1.0995574287564267,0.0", "interactions": [{"interactionpos": "-1.6964600329384885,-0.18849555921538774", "action": {"library": "H5P.GoToScene 0.1", "params": {"nextSceneId": 0}, "subContentId": "333484e1-aade-4955-b29f-95e3102d7939", "metadata": {"contentType": "Go To Scene", "license": "U", "title": "Back to the church"}}, "label": {"labelPosition": "inherit", "showLabel": "inherit"}, "labelText": "Back to the church"}, {"interactionpos": "1.4451326206513038,-0.18849555921538774", "action": {"library": "H5P.GoToScene 0.1", "params": {"nextSceneId": 2}, "subContentId": "36e198d9-e051-4329-8aa2-28cc34255c2e", "metadata": {"contentType": "Go To Scene", "license": "U", "title": "Towards Maria Lai's artwork"}}, "label": {"labelPosition": "inherit", "showLabel": "inherit"}, "labelText": "Towards Maria Lai's artwork"}]}, {"sceneId": 2, "sceneType": "360", "showBackButton": true, "iconType": "arrow", "scenename": "The path and the gateway", "scenesrc": {"path": "../content/images/scena-03.jpg", "mime": "image/jpeg", "copyright": {"license": "U"}, "width": 4096, "height": 2048}, "scenedescription": "", "cameraStartPosition": "-1.633628179866692,0.0", "interactions": [{"interactionpos": "-1.633628179866692,-0.18849555921538774", "action": {"library": "H5P.GoToScene 0.1", "params": {"nextSceneId": 3}, "subContentId": "3102d3cb-caf5-48df-9d52-3613458666f4", "metadata": {"contentType": "Go To Scene", "license": "U", "title": "Walk through the gateway"}}, "label": {"labelPosition": "inherit", "showLabel": "inherit"}, "labelText": "Walk through the gateway"}, {"interactionpos": "1.6964600329384885,-0.18849555921538774", "action": {"library": "H5P.GoToScene 0.1", "params": {"nextSceneId": 1}, "subContentId": "946aa65c-1087-451f-8318-e00c2080e345", "metadata": {"contentType": "Go To Scene", "license": "U", "title": "Back to the avenue"}}, "label": {"labelPosition": "inherit", "showLabel": "inherit"}, "labelText": "Back to the avenue"}, {"interactionpos": "-1.633628179866692,0.37699111843077515", "action": {"library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1", "params": {"text": "<p><em>Andando via</em> is a tribute to Grazia Deledda and to the women in her novels. The path begins with a large concrete gateway, an empty frame that welcomes visitors; above it, an astral geography in wrought iron stands out against the sky. Maria Lai described the gateway as &quot;an invitation to leave anxieties behind and roam into another dimension&quot;.</p><p>The astral geography recalls Maria Lai&#x27;s <em>Geografie</em>, a series developed mainly from the 1980s onwards: large pieces of fabric in which threads and embroidery draw imaginary maps, starry skies and constellations instead of real territories. In 2020 the installation was reinterpreted in 22 tapestries woven by 25 Sardinian textile workshops and exhibited at the Mudec museum in Milan.</p>"}, "subContentId": "40cb9787-bc90-4d24-9470-9e5ee338da8f", "metadata": {"contentType": "Text", "license": "U", "title": "The gateway and the astral geography"}}, "label": {"labelPosition": "inherit", "showLabel": "inherit"}, "labelText": "The gateway and the astral geography"}, {"interactionpos": "-1.0053096491487334,0.09424777960769387", "action": {"library": "H5P.Image 1.1", "params": {"decorative": false, "contentName": "Image", "expandImage": "Expand image", "minimizeImage": "Minimize image", "file": {"path": "../content/images/portale-grandangolo.jpg", "mime": "image/jpeg", "copyright": {"license": "U"}, "width": 2300, "height": 1725}, "alt": "The gateway of Andando via with the iron astral geography"}, "subContentId": "8b763a56-4cfd-43a4-bdea-6e1124299830", "metadata": {"contentType": "Image", "license": "U", "title": "The gateway up close"}}, "label": {"labelPosition": "inherit", "showLabel": "inherit"}, "labelText": "The gateway up close"}]}, {"sceneId": 3, "sceneType": "360", "showBackButton": true, "iconType": "arrow", "scenename": "The clearing", "scenesrc": {"path": "../content/images/scena-04.jpg", "mime": "image/jpeg", "copyright": {"license": "U"}, "width": 4096, "height": 2048}, "scenedescription": "", "cameraStartPosition": "2.5132741228718345,0.0", "interactions": [{"interactionpos": "2.324778563656447,-0.06283185307179592", "action": {"library": "H5P.GoToScene 0.1", "params": {"nextSceneId": 4}, "subContentId": "949b475b-d8e9-4d1e-8c6b-2a08271627fc", "metadata": {"contentType": "Go To Scene", "license": "U", "title": "To the columns"}}, "label": {"labelPosition": "inherit", "showLabel": "inherit"}, "labelText": "To the columns"}, {"interactionpos": "0.6754424205218061,-0.18849555921538774", "action": {"library": "H5P.GoToScene 0.1", "params": {"nextSceneId": 2}, "subContentId": "dd848e4a-582e-4c0a-9237-556445c7ec56", "metadata": {"contentType": "Go To Scene", "license": "U", "title": "Back to the gateway"}}, "label": {"labelPosition": "inherit", "showLabel": "inherit"}, "labelText": "Back to the gateway"}]}, {"sceneId": 4, "sceneType": "360", "showBackButton": true, "iconType": "arrow", "scenename": "Among the columns", "scenesrc": {"path": "../content/images/scena-05.jpg", "mime": "image/jpeg", "copyright": {"license": "U"}, "width": 4096, "height": 2048}, "scenedescription": "", "cameraStartPosition": "-1.2566370614359172,0.0", "interactions": [{"interactionpos": "-1.8849555921538759,-0.31415926535897926", "action": {"library": "H5P.GoToScene 0.1", "params": {"nextSceneId": 3}, "subContentId": "29877820-ca76-492c-a35a-b071dc9770ec", "metadata": {"contentType": "Go To Scene", "license": "U", "title": "Back to the clearing"}}, "label": {"labelPosition": "inherit", "showLabel": "inherit"}, "labelText": "Back to the clearing"}, {"interactionpos": "-1.2566370614359172,0.25132741228718347", "action": {"library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1", "params": {"text": "<p>The title <em>Andando via</em> means &quot;going away&quot;. The work is Maria Lai&#x27;s last public project, an open-air tribute to Grazia Deledda, the only Italian woman to have won the Nobel Prize in Literature.</p><p>Instead of a traditional monument, Maria Lai chose a modern language: eleven white concrete pillars standing in the greenery, not far from the church of the Solitudine. Walking among them feels like crossing a labyrinth or an open-air sanctuary, where the trees and the wind do most of the talking.</p>"}, "subContentId": "52bfc6ff-1d9d-46b4-8c0e-151e2bdbe002", "metadata": {"contentType": "Text", "license": "U", "title": "The columns"}}, "label": {"labelPosition": "inherit", "showLabel": "inherit"}, "labelText": "The columns"}, {"interactionpos": "1.162389281828224,0.0", "action": {"library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1", "params": {"text": "<p>On the pillars there are black metal silhouettes of women, shepherds, goats and looms, which recall the heroines of Grazia Deledda&#x27;s novels and the world they lived in. In the presentation of the work Maria Lai wrote: &quot;In every image there is man, with all his questions and his longing for the infinite.&quot;</p><p>Maria Lai compared artistic work to the everyday activities of the women of her homeland – mothers, aunts and grandmothers devoted to weaving, embroidery and crochet. For her, thread was not just a household tool but a symbolic element, able to mend tears, bring people together and give shape to thoughts.</p>"}, "subContentId": "e79c996c-1058-40f1-858e-95f86a988c09", "metadata": {"contentType": "Text", "license": "U", "title": "Grazia Deledda's women"}}, "label": {"labelPosition": "inherit", "showLabel": "inherit"}, "labelText": "Grazia Deledda's women"}, {"interactionpos": "2.246238747316702,0.15707963267948963", "action": {"library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1", "params": {"text": "<p>Writings that cannot be read are engraved in the concrete of the pillars: signs that look like hieroglyphs or the lines of an electrocardiogram.</p><p>Illegible, or asemantic, writings run through Maria Lai&#x27;s work from the 1970s onwards, for example in her sewn books. Freed from literal meaning, handwriting becomes a visual and tactile sign: in this way the artist sought a universal language that leaves the viewer&#x27;s thoughts free.</p>"}, "subContentId": "3feb0d58-c1bb-4c60-ba0a-7370232b3094", "metadata": {"contentType": "Text", "license": "U", "title": "Writings to imagine"}}, "label": {"labelPosition": "inherit", "showLabel": "inherit"}, "labelText": "Writings to imagine"}]}], "startSceneId": 0}, "behaviour": {"sceneRenderingQuality": "high", "label": {"labelPosition": "right", "showLabel": true}}, "l10n": {"title": "360° tour", "playAudioTrack": "Play audio track", "pauseAudioTrack": "Pause audio track", "sceneDescription": "Scene description", "resetCamera": "Reset view", "submitDialog": "Submit", "closeDialog": "Close", "expandButtonAriaLabel": "Expand the label", "backgroundLoading": "Loading image...", "noContent": "No content"}}